1
00:03:43,199 --> 00:03:49,199
...or never give a saga an even break!

2
00:07:26,399 --> 00:07:32,399
Blazing Saddles is a western, comedy film directed by Mel Brooks.

3
00:11:09,599 --> 00:11:15,599
A town—where everyone seems to be named
Johnson—stands in the way of the railroad.

4
00:14:52,799 --> 00:14:58,799
In order to grab their land, robber baron Hedley Lamarr sends
his henchmen to make life in the town unbearable.

5
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:42,000
After the sheriff is killed, the town demands a new sheriff from the Governor, so
Hedley convinces him to send the town the first black sheriff in the west.

6
00:22:19,199 --> 00:22:25,199
Bart, played by Cleavon Little.

7
00:26:02,399 --> 00:26:08,399
Jim, played by Gene Wilder.

8
00:29:45,599 --> 00:29:51,599
Taggart, played by Slim Pickens.

9
00:33:28,799 --> 00:33:34,799
Hedley Lamarr, played by Harvey Korman.

10
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:18,000
Lili Von Shtupp, played by Madeline Kahn.

11
00:40:55,199 --> 00:41:01,199
Governor William J. Le Petomane / Indian Chief, played by Mel Brooks.

12
00:44:38,399 --> 00:44:44,399
Blazing Saddles is a 1974 American satirical postmodernist Western comedy film directed by Mel Brooks, who co-wrote the
screenplay with Andrew Bergman, Richard Pryor, Norman Steinberg and Alan Uger, based on a story treatment by Bergman.

13
00:48:21,599 --> 00:48:27,599
The film stars Cleavon Little and Gene Wilder.

14
00:52:04,799 --> 00:52:10,799
Brooks appears in two supporting roles: Governor William J.

15
00:55:48,000 --> 00:55:54,000
Le Petomane, and a Yiddish-speaking Native American chief; he also dubs lines for
one of Lili Von Shtupp's backing troupe and a cranky moviegoer.

16
00:59:31,199 --> 00:59:37,199
The supporting cast includes Slim Pickens, Alex Karras and David Huddleston, as
well as Brooks regulars Dom DeLuise, Madeline Kahn and Harvey Korman.

17
01:03:14,399 --> 01:03:20,399
Bandleader Count Basie has a cameo as himself, appearing with his orchestra.

18
01:06:57,599 --> 01:07:03,599
The film is full of deliberate anachronisms, from the Count Basie Orchestra playing "April in
Paris" in the Wild West, to Pickens' character mentioning the Wide World of Sports.

19
01:10:40,800 --> 01:10:46,800
Blazing Saddles was released by Warner Bros.

20
01:14:24,000 --> 01:14:30,000
on February 7, 1974.

21
01:18:07,199 --> 01:18:13,199
The film received generally positive reviews from critics and audiences, was nominated for three Academy Awards and
is today regarded as a comedy classic, while grossing $119.6 million against a $2.6 million budget.

22
01:21:50,399 --> 01:21:56,399
It is ranked number six on the American Film Institute's 100 Years...100 Laughs list, and was deemed "culturally, historically, or
aesthetically significant" by the Library of Congress and was selected for preservation in the National Film Registry in 2006.

23
01:25:33,599 --> 01:25:39,599
The film explores themes of governor, saloon, gun, parody, marching band.

24
01:29:16,799 --> 01:29:22,799
The tone is lighthearted.
